| Rejected as | Count | Rule |
|---|---|---|
| {{ p['ladder_not-a-word-key'] }} | Key shorter than 3 letters, or not `^[a-z][a-z]*(-[a-z][a-z]*)*$`. |
| {{ p['ladder_label-word'] }} | In `LABEL_WORDS`: statement-environment words, months (every manuscript directory ends in one), publishers, `lean`, `openai`. |
| {{ p['ladder_maths-word'] }} | In `JUNK_WORDS` — see below. |
| {{ p['ladder_contains-digit'] }} | Any digit in the key. |
| {{ p['ladder_acronym'] }} | The dominant surface form is all capitals (`CAT`, `ADM`). |
| {{ p['ladder_latex-macro'] }} | The dominant surface form has a second internal capital (`OpenAIWidth`). |
| {{ p['ladder_no-prose-evidence'] }} | No mid-sentence capitalized occurrence anywhere in the corpus. |
| {{ p['ladder_prose-word'] }} | Fails the mid-sentence test above. |

The acronym and macro tests are judged on the **dominant surface form over all six layers**,
not on prose alone. That is a fix, not a nicety: `OpenAIWidth` reaches the candidate set
{% raw %}through a `\cite{OpenAIWidth}` that leaked into a theorem-environment title, so it never
{% endraw %}
appears as prose, and a prose-only view of surface forms cannot see it. {{ p['cat_adm a'] }}
{{ p['cat_adm b'] }}; the acronym test is what rejects them.

## The family-title escape

A name in a family title is the release's own naming decision, so it is not required to
survive the prose ladder. {{ p['escape_count'] }}: `bochner`, `campana`, `curtis`,
`elliott`, `etingof`, `green`, `jacobsthal`, `kraetzer`, `mahler`, `ostmann`, `pop`, `riesz`,
`ryser`, `shub`, `swinnerton-dyer`, `wilson`. Each appears in a family title and has no
qualifying mid-sentence prose evidence, which is a statement about how often the corpus writes
the name, not about whether it is a name.

